{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1411717-what-help-can-the-fool-arcana-provide","id":1411717,"title":"What help can The Fool Arcana provide?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-09-03T16:46:39-07:00","id":1411717,"title":"What help can The Fool Arcana provide?","content":"I have been trying to flesh out the sort of help the different Major Arcana can provide. Whether it is allowing you to buy Backgrounds like Dream or Demesne with XP, or giving you guidance, I think they all have something to offer. The Fool seems a bit trickier than most, however. <br /> <br /> I personally think what he can offer you is the starting point to any journey you want. He seems like he is the first step of the Hero\u2019s Journey, and he can help you get started with any goal you are after. This might manifest as the Destiny Background, should you wish to purchase it with XP or Flaws, but it could simply be a clue about how to be at the right place at the right time, when you will bump into a potential 5 dot Familiar Spirit who doesn\u2019t know you but needs help, or the big shot producer that sits next to you on the plane and thinks you have the right look for his movie and will give you your first big break. I suspect that every adventure he sets you on should be guaranteed to have snags and curve balls, but these things should be surmountable by one who is quick and dedicated to whatever course they are pursuing. Thoughts and/or advice?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/986-mercurial","user":"Mercurial","date":". 09-03-2020, 11:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/986-mercurial","name":"Mercurial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar986_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-03T16:59:25-07:00","id":1411719,"title":"","content":"That sounds good.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","name":"Konradleijon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23952_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-03T20:07:24-07:00","id":1411739,"title":"","content":"The archetype of the Holy Fool is a manifestation of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divine_madness\" target=\"_blank\">divine madness/&quot;crazy wisdom&quot;</a> prevalent in a number of cultural traditions.<br /> One aspect of divine madness is that of mystical revelation and prophecy, which can be associated with one's Avatar, the Dream background, and encounters with spiritual beings (Angels, faeries, eidolons, gods, etc.).<br /> Fools are also frequently held to be protected by various higher powers (God, fate, what have you), and as a result blessed with divine luck. Even if things go totally off the rails, one may find things working out for the best in the end.<br /> A combination of the two is that the Fool is traditionally able to speak truths that society or the powers that be would rather be left unsaid and actually get away with it. The Fool character in Shakespeare's King Lear is one of the more famous instances of this archetype. <br /> <br /> Also, in the Mage Tarot, the Fool card is specifically associated with Faerie and the Changelings, and to a lesser degree the madness of the Marauders. In that later context, it may provide protection against the forces of Paradox. In the former context, it might also be used as a focus for &quot;enchanting&quot; people and for the magic of Ars Cupidae (the &quot;arts of desire&quot;, ie social magic that effects hearts and minds), which also ties into traditional stories (such as Russian tales of Ivan the Fool) of characters of a kind hearted and jovial guile-less nature easily making friends and winning the hearts of others.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-04T14:57:36-07:00","id":1411898,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1411717-what-help-can-the-fool-arcana-provide?p=1411739#post1411739\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The archetype of the Holy Fool is a manifestation of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divine_madness\" target=\"_blank\">divine madness/&quot;crazy wisdom&quot;</a> prevalent in a number of cultural traditions.<br /> One aspect of divine madness is that of mystical revelation and prophecy, which can be associated with one's Avatar, the Dream background, and encounters with spiritual beings (Angels, faeries, eidolons, gods, etc.).<br /> Fools are also frequently held to be protected by various higher powers (God, fate, what have you), and as a result blessed with divine luck. Even if things go totally off the rails, one may find things working out for the best in the end.<br /> A combination of the two is that the Fool is traditionally able to speak truths that society or the powers that be would rather be left unsaid and actually get away with it. The Fool character in Shakespeare's King Lear is one of the more famous instances of this archetype.<br /> <br /> Also, in the Mage Tarot, the Fool card is specifically associated with Faerie and the Changelings, and to a lesser degree the madness of the Marauders. In that later context, it may provide protection against the forces of Paradox. In the former context, it might also be used as a focus for &quot;enchanting&quot; people and for the magic of Ars Cupidae (the &quot;arts of desire&quot;, ie social magic that effects hearts and minds), which also ties into traditional stories (such as Russian tales of Ivan the Fool) of characters of a kind hearted and jovial guile-less nature easily making friends and winning the hearts of others.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> This is exactly the feedback I wanted. Luck, easier access to divine favor, possible protection from Paradox, the power to speak truth that would be offensive from anyone else, and maybe power over the Dreaming and access to someone who has Changeling Lore 5+. This is all great, but should the possible protection from Paradox be counterbalanced with the risk of becoming a true Marauder? Perhaps The Fool, if you have made acceptable tribute to him beforehand can take your Willpower rating in Paradox, but the more he takes the higher you have to roll your willpower to avoid becoming a Marauder?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/986-mercurial","name":"Mercurial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar986_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-04T17:30:39-07:00","id":1411951,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mercurial</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1411717-what-help-can-the-fool-arcana-provide?p=1411898#post1411898\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is all great, but should the possible protection from Paradox be counterbalanced with the risk of becoming a true Marauder?</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe. It would probably depend on just what sort of protection it was providing. If it's just making it so that any possible witnesses just accept what's happening (via Mind magic just making them temporarily very gullible or extremely accepting, or some other effect), probably not so much. Likewise if it's somehow acting as a temporary Familiar to absorb Paradox (in a fashion similar to the Feast of Nettles), then the trade off may be that if the attempt is botched or the Paradox is more than can be handled, then the Mage might loose Quintessence and Willpower as if they'd had their Familiar destroyed/killed. But if the character is actively trying to warp their own view of reality and mimic some of the things Marauders do, then yes, there might be a risk of them becoming Mad, or at least entering a temporary state of Quiet. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}